		{
			"key": "en/concept/own-letters",
			"seen": "{\"title\":\"å, ä and ö are letters of their own\",\"body\":\"Swedish has 29 letters: the 26 of English, then å, ä and ö, in that order at the end of the alphabet. They are not a or o with marks, and a dictionary files them after z.\",\"sources\":[{\"title\":\"Swedish alphabet (English Wikipedia)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swedish_alphabet\"}]}",
			"verdict": "ok"
		},
		{
			"key": "en/concept/vowel-length",
			"seen": "{\"title\":\"Long and short\",\"body\":\"A stressed syllable is long: either its vowel is long, or the vowel is short and the consonant after it is long. A double consonant in spelling marks the short vowel: tak /tɑːk/, tack /takː/. Length changes the vowel too: long a is /ɑː/ and short a is /a/, long u is /ʉː/ and short u is /ɵ/. The chart shows the nine long vowels.\",\"sources\":[{\"title\":\"Engstrand (1990), Swedish, Journal of the IPA\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1017/S0025100300004060\"},{\"title\":\"Help:IPA/Swedish (English Wikipedia)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/Swedish\"}]}",
			"verdict": "ok"
		},
		{
			"key": "en/concept/sj-sound",
			"seen": "{\"title\":\"The sj-sound /ɧ/\",\"body\":\"A voiceless sound made with the back of the tongue, lips slightly rounded. It is spelled sj, skj, stj, sk before e, i, y, ä or ö, and -ti- in words such as station.\",\"sources\":[{\"title\":\"Help:IPA/Swedish (English Wikipedia)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/Swedish\"}]}",
			"verdict": "ok"
		},
		{
			"key": "en/concept/tj-sound",
			"seen": "{\"title\":\"The tj-sound /ɕ/\",\"body\":\"A soft voiceless hiss with the middle of the tongue raised, with no t in it. It is spelled tj, kj, and k before e, i, y, ä or ö: kjol, tjugo, köpa.\",\"sources\":[{\"title\":\"Help:IPA/Swedish (English Wikipedia)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/Swedish\"}]}",
			"verdict": "ok"
		},
		{
			"key": "en/concept/word-accent",
			"seen": "{\"title\":\"Two word accents\",\"body\":\"A word stressed on a syllable that another follows has one of two pitch patterns. Accent 1 is marked here with ̌ on the stressed vowel and accent 2 with ̂: anden /ˈǎndɛn/ \\\"the duck\\\", anden /ˈândɛn/ \\\"the spirit\\\". Most words of two syllables, and every compound, take accent 2. A word of one syllable has no contrast and no mark.\",\"sources\":[{\"title\":\"Engstrand (1990), Swedish, Journal of the IPA\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1017/S0025100300004060\"},{\"title\":\"Help:IPA/Swedish (English Wikipedia)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/Swedish\"}]}",
			"verdict": "ok"
		},
		{
			"key": "en/concept/everyday-numbers",
			"seen": "{\"title\":\"Numbers as they are said\",\"body\":\"The transcriptions give the careful forms. In everyday speech nio and tio are /ˈnîːɛ/ and /ˈtîːɛ/, and the tens drop their last vowel: trettio is /ˈtrɛ̂tːɪ/, fyrtio /ˈfœ̂ʈːɪ/. Hundra and tusen also come as etthundra and ettusen.\",\"sources\":[{\"title\":\"Wiktionary (Swedish entries)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Wiktionary:Main_Page\"},{\"title\":\"Wiktionary på svenska\",\"url\":\"https://sv.wiktionary.org/\"}]}",
			"verdict": "ok"
		},
		{
			"key": "en/concept/retroflex",
			"seen": "{\"title\":\"r with t, d, n, s or l\",\"body\":\"In Central Standard Swedish r merges with a following t, d, n, s or l into one sound made with the tip of the tongue raised just behind the gum ridge: ʈ, ɖ, ɳ, ʂ, ɭ. So fjorton is /ˈfjûːʈɔn/ and arton /ˈɑ̂ːʈɔn/, with no separate r.\",\"sources\":[{\"title\":\"Help:IPA/Swedish (English Wikipedia)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/Swedish\"}]}",
			"verdict": "ok"
		},
		{
			"key": "en/concept/gn-sound",
			"seen": "{\"title\":\"gn is /ŋn/\",\"body\":\"After a vowel, gn is said /ŋn/, with the ng of English \\\"sing\\\": regn /rɛŋn/, lugn.\",\"sources\":[{\"title\":\"Lexin (Swedish–English), Institutet för språk och folkminnen\",\"url\":\"https://lexin.nada.kth.se/lexin/\"},{\"title\":\"Wiktionary (Swedish entries)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Wiktionary:Main_Page\"}]}",
			"verdict": "pending"
		},
		{
			"key": "en/concept/igt-ending",
			"seen": "{\"title\":\"The ending -igt\",\"body\":\"The g of -igt is silent: soligt /ˈsûːlɪt/, molnigt, blåsigt. The ending makes the form of an adjective that goes with ett-words, and with det in det är soligt, \\\"it is sunny\\\".\",\"sources\":[{\"title\":\"Lexin (Swedish–English), Institutet för språk och folkminnen\",\"url\":\"https://lexin.nada.kth.se/lexin/\"},{\"title\":\"Wiktionary (Swedish entries)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Wiktionary:Main_Page\"}]}",
			"verdict": "pending"
		},
